asingly fear  for their safety too more than 9 000  threats were made against lawmakers in  2021 up from about 4 000 in 2017  according to the capitol police the  typical threats are a big problem  i remember the very first week that i  started covering the hill for the wall  street journal was the week that gabby  giffords got shot and  there's a sense that instead of stepping  back from that sort of precipice  the country is only  deepening its entanglement with that  sort of physical violence  congress's divide reflects the country's  split in a november wsj poll 57 of  republicans said the election was stolen  from trump compared to just 3 percent of  democrats  this divide could have implications for  both parties on what will happen in this  year's midterm elections for republicans  there's going to be a first test of who  wins in the republican primaries the  trump true believers or the more  conventional republicans who want to  move on and for democrats  there's the second question of whether  democrats can or will use this debate uh  in the general election try to use it to  their benefit by saying essentially this  is dangerous  you shouldn't be electing people who  question the results of national  elections i think democrats have some  deciding to do here about whether they  want to continue to focus on this  subject or  move on in their own way  some house members aren't even going to  face that choice 25 democrats and 11  republicans have said they will retire  or seek another office  republicans are favored to win the house  and potentially win the senate if the  gop takes control the party will also  gain control of the january 6th  committee it's not just possible that if  republicans win control in 2022 they  will dissolve the committee it's likely  that house republicans will dissolve  that january 6 committee if they win  control of the public for the country  the debate over january 6th and the 2020  results has created an issue that could  have a more lasting impact in the  november wsj poll 64 of republicans and  42 percent of voters overall said they  did not have confidence in the election  system if one of the effects of this  this being the election in january 6th  is that that confidence is permanently  eroded then i think we're headed for  dangerous territory it doesn't mean it's  permanent but right now i'd say the  warning signs are flashing  [Music]
 

